---
/**
 * website/src/pages/security.astro — the security page (website plan, Task W1.8; the
 * owner's brief and ruling D189 of 2026-10-19, and the boundary diagram of 2026-10-20).
 *
 * **The page is four things, and nothing else**: three of them are the document's own words,
 * and the fourth is the page's own drawing written in them.
 *
 *   1. the **boundary diagram** (the owner's brief of 2026-10-20): one inline SVG of the
 *      trust boundaries, the same facts as a list under it, and the two limits the document's
 *      own summary names below that — every label a phrase of `SECURITY.md`, proved while the
 *      page builds (`quoted()` below);
 *   2. the **summary**, which is `SECURITY.md`'s own "In short" section, rendered from the
 *      generated copy of the document;
 *   3. the **"Reporting a vulnerability" section**, rendered from the same copy;
 *   4. a **link to `SECURITY.md`** in the repository.
 *
 * The page used to render `SECURITY.md` in full, behind a contents list built from its
 * headings. The owner cut it to three things, and then asked for the boundary diagram above
 * them (2026-10-20). D189 settled the one open question —
 * where the summary comes from: **each long page's summary is rendered from a file, never
 * written on the page**, and for this page the file is `SECURITY.md` itself, whose short
 * "In short" section is the summary the page renders, written or approved by the owner as
 * every `SECURITY.md` change is (`docs/website/redesign/reconciliation.md`, D189). So the
 * page carries no summary prose of its own: a sentence here that the document does not state
 * is the one thing a security page may not have (D99), and there is now nowhere to write one.
 *
 * Six decisions are visible in the file:
 *
 *   1. **Both sections are the document's, word for word under the renderer.** They are
 *      extracted from the generated `website/content/security.md` — the file `go run
 *      ./internal/clidoc` writes from `SECURITY.md`, which `loadSite()` validates and a
 *      missing or hand-edited copy of stops the build (W1.3 step 2) — so what the page says
 *      cannot drift from the file it links to. A document that renamed, moved or emptied
 *      either section stops the build rather than shipping a page with a hole in it.
 *      `website/test/security.mjs` holds both to the document, bullet for bullet and
 *      paragraph for paragraph.
 *   2. **The diagram is the document's words too, and the build is what holds it there.** A
 *      picture of a trust boundary is a claim about security, so every label of it — the
 *      drawing's accessible name, each line inside the drawing, each fact under it and each of
 *      the two lines below them — goes through `quoted()`: the phrase is looked for in
 *      `SECURITY.md` while the page builds, and a phrase the document does not carry stops the
 *      build (D99). The owner's brief for the picture named two things the document does not
 *      say — that the pull request is a **draft** the owner **merges**, and that the container
 *      is in **your own cloud project** — and a label that would need them is a label the page
 *      does not carry: the drawing says the document's own "ends every run with a pull
 *      request", and the container is the document's "one container per run".
 *   3. **The drawing arranges the document's words, and the arrangement is a decision no gate
 *      reads.** Every label being the document's own is what the build and
 *      `website/test/security.mjs` prove; what the labels *say together* is drawn by hand, and
 *      a picture can put two of the document's sentences side by side into a claim the
 *      document makes only as a conditional. The model-key card is the one place that could
 *      happen, so it is drawn as the document states it: `SECURITY.md`'s "What is not on by
 *      default" bullet is one sentence — the budget gateway is **not on by default**, so the
 *      real model key **sits in the agent's environment** — and its "With the budget gateway
 *      on, model keys stay out of the agent's environment" is the other half of that same
 *      conditional, never a second fact beside the first. So the card's title names the
 *      gateway, its first line the default, and its last two lines the key and where it sits:
 *      one state, the default one, which is the state the facts under the picture state too.
 *      Drawing "the budget gateway" and "sits in the agent's environment" as two co-equal
 *      lines would assert both halves of the conditional at once, and no test here reads a
 *      picture's arrangement — which is why the arrangement is written down.
 *   4. **A cross-reference inside the document becomes a link into the document.** The "In
 *      short" bullets each end by naming the section they come from
 *      (`([Known limits](#known-limits))`), and the page renders two of those sections and no
 *      others. A reference to one of the two the page carries — today the summary's
 *      `[Reporting a vulnerability](#reporting-a-vulnerability)` — is a link to the page's own
 *      anchor, because that is where the section is and where a reader can follow it; a
 *      reference to any other section is rewritten to the document in the repository at the
 *      section it names. A cross-reference the document has **no heading for** is rendered as
 *      its own text and
 *      not as a link at all (round 4 of the review): every target the page can build for it
 *      is wrong — the fragment as written is a link that goes nowhere, `website/test/
 *      linkcheck.mjs` refuses a GitHub link whose fragment is not a heading, and the
 *      document's own URL sends the reader to the top of the file with a link that reads
 *      like every other one and arrives nowhere near the section it names. Text promises
 *      nothing. `SECURITY.md` ships one (`[Money](#money)`, whose target is a bold lead in a
 *      bullet under "Known limits" and not a heading); the owner has it in the pull request,
 *      because the fix is a `SECURITY.md` edit. `website/test/security.mjs` pins the whole
 *      rule over the built page — every cross-reference's text and order against the
 *      document's own links, its href against the heading its fragment names (the page's own
 *      anchor where the page renders that heading, the document's URL otherwise), and the
 *      fragment-less one against the fact that it is not a link — so a fragment dropped,
 *      aimed at another heading, sent to GitHub though the page carries it, or rendered as an
 *      anchor fails there.
 *   5. **The link is the repository's, and its path is read rather than typed.** The URL is
 *      built from `site.yaml`'s repository and the generated file's own `source:` header, so
 *      the page cannot point at a document this repository does not carry.
 *   6. **The styles are global and token-only.** The rendered sections arrive through
 *      `set:html`, which Astro's scoped styles do not reach, so the block below is
 *      `is:global` and every selector is prefixed with the page's own marker attribute so it
 *      cannot reach another page. Colours come from W1.2's semantic tokens and never from a
 *      literal here.
 *
 * Nothing in this file fetches anything, reads a clock or starts a process: the page is
 * built from the files in this repository (Global Constraints 4 and 5). The diagram is one
 * inline `<svg>`: the drawing loads nothing — no image file, no font file, no script of its
 * own — and the page adds no script of its own either. The site's one first-party file,
 * `public/scripts/site.js`, is the layout's and loads on every page (the layout is held to
 * that by `website/test/layout.mjs`); nothing in it reads the drawing, which is markup a
 * browser paints.
 */
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'
import { loadSite } from '../content.ts'
import { headingAnchor, headingText } from '../lib/github-slug.mjs'

// The whole generated layer, validated: a missing or hand-edited security.md, or any other
// fact the site renders, fails here with the file's name in the message (see 1 above).
const site = loadSite()
const securityDocument = site.documents['security.md']

// The link's target: the file this page renders, in the repository `site.yaml` names. The
// path is the generated file's own `source:` fact, so neither the repository nor the path is
// written here, and `main` is the branch those files live on — the repository's default
// branch, which every other GitHub link the site builds also names.
const sourceHref = new URL(`blob/main/${securityDocument.source}`, `${site.facts.repository}/`).href

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The document's own sections, and the markup they are rendered with.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

/** The two sections the page renders, as the document spells their headings. */
const SUMMARY = 'In short'
const REPORTING = 'Reporting a vulnerability'
const SUMMARY_ID = 'in-short'
const REPORTING_ID = 'reporting-a-vulnerability'

/**
 * The document's lines under its `## <heading>`, up to the next heading of the same level or
 * higher. A document without the heading fails the build: the page renders both sections and
 * has nothing to fall back on, so a document that moved one stops the build rather than
 * shipping a page with a hole in it.
 */
function sectionLines(heading: string): string[] {
  const lines = securityDocument.body.split('\n')
  const start = lines.findIndex((line) => line.trim() === `## ${heading}`)
  if (start === -1) {
    throw new Error(
      `${securityDocument.path} has no "## ${heading}" heading, and this page renders that section of it. The document is the page's whole text (D189), so a document that moved the section stops the build.`,
    )
  }
  const rest = lines.slice(start + 1)
  const end = rest.findIndex((line) => /^#{1,2}[ \t]/.test(line))
  return end === -1 ? rest : rest.slice(0, end)
}

/** The anchors the document's own headings carry, the way GitHub slugs them. */
const documentAnchors = new Set(
  securityDocument.body
    .split('\n')
    .map((line) => /^#{1,6}[ \t]+(.*)$/.exec(line.trim()))
    .filter((match): match is RegExpExecArray => match !== null)
    .map((match) => headingAnchor(headingText(match[1]))),
)

/**
 * The anchors **this page itself renders**: the two sections it carries, under the ids the
 * markup above gives their headings. A cross-reference to one of them is a link a reader can
 * follow here, and it must be the page's own anchor rather than the document's — the section
 * is on this page, and sending the reader to GitHub for a section they are looking at is the
 * wrong destination even though the fragment would resolve there too.
 */
const pageAnchors = new Set([SUMMARY_ID, REPORTING_ID])

/**
 * A link target in the document, as the page renders it. A path is the document's own and
 * is left as it is; a cross-reference into a section **this page renders** becomes a link to
 * the page's own anchor, which is the section a reader can reach from here (the summary's
 * `[Reporting a vulnerability](#reporting-a-vulnerability)` is that case, and it is the page's
 * own `<h2 id="reporting-a-vulnerability">` that answers it); a cross-reference into any other
 * section of the document becomes a link to the document in the repository, at the section it
 * names. A fragment the document has **no heading for** has no target at all (`null`), and
 * `inline()` renders that reference as its own text: the only link the page could build for it
 * would send a reader to the top of the file, or to a fragment nothing answers, which is a
 * promise the page cannot keep.
 */
function target(raw: string): string | null {
  if (!raw.startsWith('#')) return raw
  const fragment = raw.slice(1)
  if (pageAnchors.has(fragment)) return `#${fragment}`
  return documentAnchors.has(fragment) ? `${sourceHref}#${fragment}` : null
}

/** The document's inline markup, as HTML. */
function escapeHTML(text: string): string {
  return text
    .replace(/&/g, '&amp;')
    .replace(/</g, '&lt;')
    .replace(/>/g, '&gt;')
    .replace(/"/g, '&quot;')
}

/**
 * One line's inline markup: code spans, bold, italics and links, in the order the document
 * nests them, with everything else escaped — and nothing added, so a space the document
 * writes is a space the page renders. The subset is what the document's two sections use and
 * nothing else is guessed at, because a page that mangles a security sentence is worse than
 * one that does not render it (D99).
 */
function inline(text: string): string {
  let html = ''
  let plain = ''
  const flush = (): void => {
    html += escapeHTML(plain)
    plain = ''
  }

  let i = 0
  while (i < text.length) {
    const rest = text.slice(i)
    let match: RegExpExecArray | null = null

    if (text[i] === '`' && (match = /^`([^`]+)`/.exec(rest)) !== null) {
      flush()
      html += `<code>${escapeHTML(match[1])}</code>`
      i += match[0].length
      continue
    }
    if (rest.startsWith('**') && (match = /^\*\*(.+?)\*\*/.exec(rest)) !== null) {
      flush()
      html += `<strong>${inline(match[1])}</strong>`
      i += match[0].length
      continue
    }
    if (text[i] === '*' && (match = /^\*([^*]+)\*/.exec(rest)) !== null) {
      flush()
      html += `<em>${inline(match[1])}</em>`
      i += match[0].length
      continue
    }
    if (text[i] === '[' && (match = /^\[([^\]]*)\]\(([^)\s]+)\)/.exec(rest)) !== null) {
      flush()
      // The document's own link: its text is rendered as it stands and its target is read
      // through `target()` above. A reference the document has no heading for renders as
      // that text alone — the word, without a promise of where it goes (decision 2).
      const href = target(match[2])
      html += href === null ? inline(match[1]) : `<a href="${escapeHTML(href)}">${inline(match[1])}</a>`
      i += match[0].length
      continue
    }

    plain += text[i]
    i++
  }

  flush()
  return html
}

/**
 * A section's bullets, in the document's order, each one line joined back together when the
 * file wrapped it. A wrap is a line that directly follows a bullet or another wrapped line; a
 * line that follows a **blank** line is a paragraph of its own, and it stops the build, as does
 * a line before the first bullet: the summary is the document's "In short" list, and a
 * paragraph added under it is text the page would otherwise render inside the last bullet's
 * `<li>`, which is text of the owner's the page has no business reshaping (D189).
 */
function bulletLines(heading: string): string[] {
  const bullets: string[] = []
  // Whether the line before this one was a bullet or a wrap of one. A blank line breaks the
  // run, so the line after it is a block of its own rather than a continuation.
  let wrapping = false
  for (const line of sectionLines(heading)) {
    const bullet = /^\s*[-*][ \t]+(.*)$/.exec(line)
    if (bullet !== null) {
      bullets.push(bullet[1].trim())
      wrapping = true
    } else if (line.trim() === '') {
      wrapping = false
    } else if (wrapping) {
      bullets[bullets.length - 1] += ` ${line.trim()}`
    } else {
      throw new Error(
        `${securityDocument.path}'s "## ${heading}" section carries a line that is not a bullet and does not wrap one (${JSON.stringify(line.trim())}), and this page renders the section as the list it is. The document is the page's whole text (D189), so a paragraph beside the list stops the build rather than being rendered as part of the bullet above it.`,
      )
    }
  }
  if (bullets.length === 0) {
    throw new Error(
      `${securityDocument.path}'s "## ${heading}" section carries no bullet, and this page renders it as the list it is: an empty summary is a page that describes nothing (D189).`,
    )
  }
  return bullets
}

/** The summary: the document's own "In short" bullets, each rendered as HTML. */
const summaryBullets = bulletLines(SUMMARY).map(inline)

/**
 * The reporting section's paragraphs, each rendered: the section is two of them today. An
 * emptied section stops the build for the same reason the summary's does — the page would
 * otherwise render a heading over nothing, and a reporter following it would find no
 * instructions where the document promised them (D189).
 */
const reportingParagraphs = sectionLines(REPORTING)
  .join('\n')
  .split(/\n[ \t]*\n/)
  .map((paragraph) => paragraph.replace(/\s+/g, ' ').trim())
  .filter((paragraph) => paragraph !== '')
  .map(inline)

if (reportingParagraphs.length === 0) {
  throw new Error(
    `${securityDocument.path}'s "## ${REPORTING}" section carries no paragraph, and this page renders that section as the document writes it: an emptied section is a heading over nothing, and the page has no fallback text of its own (D189).`,
  )
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The boundary diagram (the owner's brief of 2026-10-20), in the document's own words.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

/**
 * `SECURITY.md` as a phrase is looked for in it: its link syntax reduced to the words that
 * carry it, its inline markers removed, every run of whitespace collapsed to one space, in
 * lower case. A label is compared against this and not against the file, so a line the
 * document wrapped or a code span it marked is not read as a difference in what it says —
 * and nothing else is normalized, because the words are the whole point: a word the document
 * does not carry is a word this page may not render (D99).
 */
const documentText = securityDocument.body
  .replace(/\[([^\]]*)\]\([^)]*\)/g, '$1')
  .replace(/[*`]/g, '')
  .replace(/\s+/g, ' ')
  .toLowerCase()

/** The same reading of a label the page renders, for the comparison above. */
function normalized(text: string): string {
  return text.replace(/[*`]/g, '').replace(/\s+/g, ' ').toLowerCase()
}

/**
 * A phrase of the document, proved to be one while the page builds. Every label of the
 * boundary diagram goes through this: the page carries the document's own words and no others
 * (D99), so a label that drifts from the file — a word changed, or a claim the document does
 * not make — stops the build rather than shipping a sentence about security that the policy
 * does not state. Case is the page's own (a heading capitalizes its first word); the words
 * are not.
 */
function quoted(text: string): string {
  if (!documentText.includes(normalized(text))) {
    throw new Error(
      `${securityDocument.path} does not say ${JSON.stringify(text)}, and this page renders it as a label of its boundary diagram. The page carries the document's own words and no others (D99), so the label goes or the document changes — and a change to the document is the owner's.`,
    )
  }
  return text
}

/**
 * One label of the drawing, in the document's words, ending with the space that separates it
 * from the label after it.
 *
 * The space is not drawn and is not a typo. SVG strips a line's trailing whitespace before it
 * lays the line out, and each label here is positioned by its own `x`/`y` rather than
 * following the one before it. It is there for the site's spacing gate (`test/spacing.mjs`,
 * round 3 of this page's review), which reads two adjacent runs of text with no space between
 * them as one word: two labels of this drawing are two runs of text, so without it the gate
 * reads "the agent" followed by "permission prompts turned off" as "agentpermission" — the
 * defect that gate exists for, reported on a picture where no reader can see it. Every label
 * ends with it, uniformly, so the rule is one rule.
 */
function diagramLabel(text: string): string {
  return `${quoted(text)} `
}

/** The heading the drawing sits under: the document's own sentence for the boundary. */
const DIAGRAM_HEADING = quoted('The trust boundary is the container')

/**
 * The drawing's accessible name: the document's one-sentence summary of the design, which is
 * what the picture draws. A screen reader reads this name, and then the list under the
 * picture, which carries the same facts as words.
 */
const DIAGRAM_LABEL = quoted(
  'Fugaro runs a coding agent with its permission prompts turned off, in a fresh cloud container per run, and ends every run with a pull request',
)

/**
 * The facts under the picture, as the document's own clauses: who sends the task, what the
 * run is, whose accounts the per-repository identity runs under, and where the model key sits.
 * Each is rendered with the page's own inline markup, so a code span in the document is a
 * `<code>` here exactly as it is in the summary below.
 */
const DIAGRAM_FACTS = [
  'The design assumes you trust the repository and the source of the task: anyone who can push to it, launch a run, or is listed in `followup.trusted` can steer an agent that holds the credentials below',
  'Fugaro runs a coding agent with its permission prompts turned off, in a fresh cloud container per run, and ends every run with a pull request',
  "Each repository's jobs and builds run as their own service accounts",
  "What is not on by default: the budget gateway, so with no `budget:` block the real model key sits in the agent's environment",
].map((fact) => inline(quoted(fact)))

/**
 * The two lines under it: what the boundaries stop and what nothing stops, each the
 * document's own summary bullet, whole. The cross-reference a bullet ends with is not part of
 * its sentence, so it is not part of the line.
 */
const DIAGRAM_LIMITS = [
  'What limits the blast radius today: one container per run, per-repository identities, and narrow git credentials — a project-wide credential is not narrow',
  "What no boundary stops: network egress is unrestricted, the agent shares the container with the runner and can read its `/proc`, and the caps are the gateway's — set a credit limit on every key you give Fugaro",
].map((line) => inline(quoted(line)))
---

<Base
  title="Security"
  description="Fugaro's security model in short, the document's own instructions for reporting a vulnerability, and a link to the full policy."
>
  <article class="security" data-security-page>
    <h1>Security</h1>

    {/* The boundary diagram (the owner's brief of 2026-10-20): one inline SVG of the trust
        boundaries, the same facts as a list under it, and the two limits below that. Every
        label is a phrase of SECURITY.md, proved while the page builds (`quoted()` above), and
        the drawing is one picture with a name: `role="img"` and an aria-label, so a screen
        reader reads the name and then the list rather than every label of the drawing as a
        loose string of its own. No script reads it and none is loaded for it. */}
    <section data-security-diagram>
      <h2>{DIAGRAM_HEADING}</h2>
      <figure>
        <svg
          class="boundary-drawing"
          viewBox="0 0 360 688"
          role="img"
          aria-label={DIAGRAM_LABEL}
          focusable="false"
        >
          {/* Who sends the task: the repository, and the people the document trusts with it.
              The phrase is the longest the drawing draws and it is broken where it is broken
              because of that: at the estimate the gate measures with, the breaks the first cut
              and its first repair used (after "run,", then after "launch") are each wider than
              this card's content box, and this one fits it. The lines of a drawing are
              hand-placed and the gate re-measures them. */}
          <rect class="boundary-card" data-pad="16" x="12" y="12" width="336" height="92" rx="12" />
          <text class="boundary-title" font-size="13" x="28" y="42">{diagramLabel('the source of the task')}</text>
          <text class="boundary-line" font-size="13" x="28" y="66">{diagramLabel('anyone who can push to it, launch a')}</text>
          <text class="boundary-line" font-size="13" x="28" y="88">{diagramLabel('run, or is listed in followup.trusted')}</text>

          <g class="boundary-arrow">
            <line x1="180" y1="104" x2="180" y2="121" />
            <polygon class="boundary-arrow-head" points="180,130 174,119 186,119" />
          </g>

          {/* The boundary itself: one container per run, and what the document says is inside
              it. The dashed box is the boundary, so nothing is drawn crossing it except the
              two arrows that leave it. */}
          <rect class="boundary-boundary" data-pad="20" x="12" y="130" width="336" height="424" rx="16" />
          <text class="boundary-heading" font-size="20" x="32" y="166">{diagramLabel('one container per run')}</text>

          <rect class="boundary-card" data-pad="16" x="32" y="182" width="288" height="72" rx="10" />
          <text class="boundary-title" font-size="13" x="48" y="208">{diagramLabel('the agent')}</text>
          <text class="boundary-line" font-size="13" x="48" y="232">{diagramLabel('permission prompts turned off')}</text>

          <rect class="boundary-card" data-pad="16" x="32" y="262" width="288" height="72" rx="10" />
          <text class="boundary-title" font-size="13" x="48" y="288">{diagramLabel('the runner')}</text>
          <text class="boundary-line" font-size="13" x="48" y="310">{diagramLabel('the agent shares the container')}</text>
          <text class="boundary-line" font-size="13" x="48" y="328">{diagramLabel('can read its /proc')}</text>

          <rect class="boundary-card" data-pad="16" x="32" y="342" width="288" height="96" rx="10" />
          <text class="boundary-title" font-size="13" x="48" y="368">{diagramLabel('per-repository identities')}</text>
          <text class="boundary-line" font-size="13" x="48" y="392">{diagramLabel('their own service accounts')}</text>
          <text class="boundary-line" font-size="13" x="48" y="416">{diagramLabel('narrow git credentials')}</text>

          {/* The key the container holds, and the gateway that would keep it out of the agent's
              environment — the document's own conditional, in the order it states it: the
              gateway is *not on by default*, so the real key sits in the agent's environment.
              The two are one sentence and not two facts, so they are drawn as one: the card's
              title names the gateway, its first line the default, and its last two lines the
              key and where it sits. Drawing "the budget gateway" beside "sits in the agent's
              environment" as two co-equal lines would be a conjunction the document never
              states (SECURITY.md:64 and :72 are the two halves of one conditional), and the
              arrangement is the one thing about this drawing no gate reads — the gate measures
              each label against the box it is drawn in, and cannot read what the boxes mean. */}
          <rect class="boundary-card" data-pad="16" x="32" y="446" width="288" height="96" rx="10" />
          <text class="boundary-title" font-size="13" x="48" y="472">{diagramLabel('the budget gateway')}</text>
          <text class="boundary-line" font-size="13" x="48" y="494">{diagramLabel('not on by default')}</text>
          <text class="boundary-line" font-size="13" x="48" y="512">{diagramLabel('the real model key')}</text>
          <text class="boundary-line" font-size="13" x="48" y="530">{diagramLabel("sits in the agent's environment")}</text>

          {/* Out of the container: the pull request every run ends with. */}
          <g class="boundary-arrow">
            <line x1="150" y1="554" x2="150" y2="571" />
            <polygon class="boundary-arrow-head" points="150,580 144,569 156,569" />
          </g>
          <rect class="boundary-card" data-pad="16" x="12" y="580" width="318" height="64" rx="12" />
          <text class="boundary-title" font-size="13" x="28" y="618">{diagramLabel('ends every run with a pull request')}</text>

          {/* And out of the boundary, past it: the egress the document says nothing stops. */}
          <g class="boundary-arrow">
            <path d="M330 528 H 342 V 631" />
            <polygon class="boundary-arrow-head" points="342,640 336,629 348,629" />
          </g>
